---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import FAQList from '../components/FAQList.astro';
import PlanningCTA from '../components/PlanningCTA.astro';
import { images } from '../data/assets';
import { activePages } from '../data/landing';
const breadcrumbs=[{label:'Trekking & Active Travel',href:'/trekking-active-travel/'}];
const routes=[
  {title:'Supported Multi-Day Trekking',copy:'Point-to-point, lodge-based, hotel-based, or mixed journeys where the route itself is a central part of the experience.',image:images.trekking,tags:['Trails','Multi-day','Groups'],href:'/trekking-active-travel/supported-trekking/'},
  {title:'Beginner-Friendly Active Travel',copy:'Trips that combine meaningful activity with realistic pacing, comfortable recovery, and clear preparation for travelers new to active journeys.',image:images.lake,tags:['Beginner-friendly','Active rest','Comfort'],href:'/trekking-active-travel/beginner-active-trips/'},
  {title:'Mountain & Peak Programs',copy:'Mountain travel built around appropriate objectives, local expertise, acclimatization, weather, and group readiness rather than summit marketing alone.',image:images.fuji,tags:['Mountains','Objectives','Readiness'],href:'/plan-a-trip/'},
  {title:'Active Cultural Travel',copy:'Walk, hike, paddle, cycle, or move through a region while keeping food, communities, history, and local context central to the journey.',image:images.marrakech,tags:['Culture','Movement','Discovery'],href:'/cultural-cruise-travel/cultural-group-travel/'}
];
const faqs=[
  {q:'How difficult are Talon Expeditions trekking trips?',a:'Difficulty varies by itinerary. We describe the practical demands of a trip using factors such as daily distance, elevation gain and loss, altitude, terrain, consecutive active days, pack requirements, weather exposure, and available support. The goal is to give travelers enough information to judge fit instead of relying only on labels such as moderate or challenging.'},
  {q:'Do trekking trips always require a guide?',a:'No. Some destinations or activities require licensed guides, while other routes can be appropriate for experienced independent groups with lighter logistical support. The support model depends on local rules, route complexity, group experience, and the amount of responsibility the group wants to carry.'},
  {q:'Can a private group include different fitness levels?',a:'Often yes, when the route and local operation allow options. That can mean shorter alternatives, luggage support, different pace groups, recovery time, or a clearly defined minimum ability. The differences should be addressed during planning rather than discovered after booking.'},
  {q:'Can active trips still use comfortable hotels?',a:'Yes. Active travel is not defined by sleeping rough. Many groups prefer demanding days combined with comfortable hotels, lodges, good meals, private transfers, and meaningful recovery.'},
  {q:'How should travelers prepare for a trekking trip?',a:'Preparation depends on the route but may include progressive walking or hiking, time on feet, strength, appropriate footwear, equipment testing, document and insurance checks, and activity-specific guidance. High-altitude or technical trips may require additional preparation and professional advice.'}
];
const serviceSchema={'@type':'Service',name:'Trekking and Active Group Travel',description:'Supported trekking, hiking, mountain and active group travel planning with itinerary design, logistics, local specialist coordination, and traveler preparation.',provider:{'@id':`${Astro.site}#organization`},areaServed:{'@type':'Place',name':'Worldwide'}};
const faqSchema={'@type':'FAQPage',mainEntity:faqs.map(i=>({'@type':'Question',name:i.q,acceptedAnswer:{'@type':'Answer',text:i.a}}))};
---
<BaseLayout title="Trekking & Active Group Travel | Talon Expeditions" description="Supported trekking, hiking, mountain and active group travel for clubs, communities and private groups, with route planning, local specialists, logistics, and traveler preparation." image={images.trekking} breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs} schema={[serviceSchema,faqSchema]}>
  <section class="page-hero" style={`background-image:url('${images.trekking}')`}><div class="shell"><Breadcrumbs items={breadcrumbs}/><p class="eyebrow">Trekking & Active Travel</p><h1 class="section-title">Move through the place instead of only looking at it.</h1><p>Supported hiking, trekking, mountain, and active journeys where route difficulty, pacing, accommodation, local expertise, logistics, and traveler preparation are designed around the people actually going.</p></div></section>

  <section class="section"><div class="shell definition-grid"><div class="definition-sticky"><p class="eyebrow">Direct answer</p><h2>What is supported active travel?</h2></div><div class="definition-body"><div class="answer-panel"><p>Supported active travel is a trip where hiking, trekking, paddling, cycling, skiing, climbing, diving, or another physical activity is a meaningful part of experiencing the destination, while the practical travel structure is coordinated around the group. Support can include route design, accommodation, transport, local guides or operators where appropriate, equipment planning, luggage movement, contingency time, and preparation before departure.</p></div><h2>Active does not have to mean extreme.</h2><p>The same category can include a first multi-day hiking trip with comfortable hotels, a lodge-to-lodge trek, a high-altitude route, an active cultural itinerary, or a more demanding mountain objective. What matters is that the trip describes the real physical demands and gives travelers enough information to decide whether the experience fits them.</p><p>For private groups, that assessment begins before the route is finalized. A hiking club may want long days and simple lodging. A social group may want shorter active blocks with more comfort and culture. A mixed-experience group may need optional routes, luggage support, or a slower first day. Those are itinerary decisions, not afterthoughts.</p></div></div></section>

  <section class="specialty-intro soft-section"><div class="shell"><div class="specialty-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">Ways to travel actively</p><h2 class="section-title">Choose the challenge that fits the group.</h2></div><p class="section-copy">The route is important, but so are recovery, comfort, local rules, transport, equipment, weather, and what the group wants to experience when it is not moving.</p></div><div class="destination-grid">{routes.map(item=><a class="destination-card" href={item.href} style={`background-image:url('${item.image}')`}><div class="destination-content"><span class="tagline">Active journey</span><h3>{item.title}</h3><p>{item.copy}</p><div class="mini-tags">{item.tags.map(tag=><span>{tag}</span>)}</div></div></a>)}</div></div></section>

  <section class="section"><div class="shell"><div class="detail-gallery"><div class="gallery-main" style={`background-image:url('${images.lake}')`}></div><div style={`background-image:url('${images.road}')`}></div><div style={`background-image:url('${images.backpacker}')`}></div></div><div class="detail-body"><aside><div class="trip-facts"><h3>Active-trip planning factors</h3><div class="fact"><strong>Daily demand</strong><span>Distance, elevation, terrain, altitude, pack weight, and time moving.</span></div><div class="fact"><strong>Recovery</strong><span>Consecutive days, rest, sleep, meals, transfers, and accommodation.</span></div><div class="fact"><strong>Local support</strong><span>Guides, permits, transport, luggage, technical providers, and destination rules.</span></div><div class="fact"><strong>Traveler readiness</strong><span>Fitness, equipment, skills, documents, insurance, and expectations before departure.</span></div></div></aside><div class="overview-panel"><p class="eyebrow">What a strong itinerary includes</p><h2>The route, recovery, logistics, and support all have to agree with each other.</h2><p>A beautiful trail is not enough. The route has to work with travel days, accommodation, weather, local transport, group pace, meals, luggage, and the number of active days travelers can realistically sustain. When those elements are designed separately, small problems compound quickly.</p><div class="check-grid"><span>Daily distance and elevation described clearly</span><span>Realistic transfer and start times</span><span>Season, weather, and daylight considered</span><span>Optionality where mixed abilities need it</span><span>Equipment and packing expectations defined</span><span>Recovery and accommodation matched to effort</span><span>Guide or operator roles explained</span><span>Contingency for delays or changing conditions</span></div><h3>Example journey rhythm</h3><div class="day-grid"><div class="day-card"><span class="day-label">Build in</span><h3>Arrive & orient</h3><p>Settle in, check equipment, move lightly, and give the group enough time to arrive physically as well as geographically.</p></div><div class="day-card"><span class="day-label">Main effort</span><h3>Go long</h3><p>Use the strongest active days for the route segments that define the journey, with the transport and local support already in place.</p></div><div class="day-card"><span class="day-label">Recover</span><h3>Discover & reset</h3><p>Use lighter movement, culture, food, or rest to absorb the place and allow the group to sustain the itinerary rather than simply survive it.</p></div></div></div></div></div></section>

  <section class="section dark-section"><div class="shell mission-grid"><div><p class="eyebrow">Group readiness</p><h2 class="section-title">A stronger group at departure has a better trip on the ground.</h2><p style="color:#aaa">Preparation is not about turning every traveler into an athlete. It is about making the actual demands visible early enough that people can train, test equipment, resolve documents, and make an informed decision about the trip.</p><a class="button" href="/resources/trip-preparation/">Trip Preparation Resources</a></div><div class="mission-stack"><div class="mission-card"><h3>Describe difficulty practically</h3><p>Use distance, elevation, altitude, terrain, time on feet, consecutive days, and support rather than vague labels alone.</p></div><div class="mission-card"><h3>Prepare for the repeated effort</h3><p>A traveler may be able to complete one difficult day but still need preparation for doing it again after limited recovery.</p></div><div class="mission-card"><h3>Test equipment before departure</h3><p>Footwear, packs, layers, specialist equipment, and personal systems should not be new on the first important day of the trip.</p></div></div></div></section>

  <section class="section"><div class="shell"><div class="specialty-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">Active-travel paths</p><h2 class="section-title">Go deeper before choosing the route.</h2></div><p class="section-copy">These pages separate different traveler needs so a beginner-friendly active trip does not get described like a technical mountain program.</p></div><div class="category-card-grid">{activePages.map(page=><a class="category-card" href={`/trekking-active-travel/${page.slug}/`}><div class="category-card-image" style={`background-image:url('${page.hero}')`}></div><div class="category-card-body"><h3>{page.title}</h3><p>{page.description}</p><span>Explore this trip type →</span></div></a>)}<a class="category-card" href="/group-travel/hiking-clubs/"><div class="category-card-image" style={`background-image:url('${images.backpacker}')`}></div><div class="category-card-body"><h3>Group Travel for Hiking Clubs</h3><p>Planning for clubs and communities that care about route quality, difficulty, member fit, and outdoor experience more than generic sightseeing.</p><span>See the club planning model →</span></div></a></div></div></section>

  <section class="section soft-section"><div class="shell"><div class="specialty-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">Common questions</p><h2 class="section-title">What travelers ask before an active trip.</h2></div><p class="section-copy">The actual trip page and local provider requirements remain the authority for difficulty, safety, equipment, and prerequisites.</p></div><FAQList items={faqs} openFirst={true}/></div></section>

  <PlanningCTA title="Tell us the challenge your group is ready for." copy="Start with the activity, route idea, destination, or simply the level of challenge you want. We can help determine what kind of active itinerary fits the group." />
</BaseLayout>
